1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,216
The school respiratory sequence builds on Week One assessment. Its
purpose is to connect what you see with the physiological problem,
choose support and discover promptly whether that support works.

2
00:00:12,216 --> 00:00:25,752
A patient can have an open airway but inadequate ventilation, or
adequate ventilation but poor oxygen transfer. Naming the failing
function is more useful than simply announcing a breathing problem.

3
00:00:25,752 --> 00:00:37,056
Start with immediate threats and obtain help early. Do not postpone
opening an obstructed airway or assisting inadequate breathing while
completing an extensive respiratory history.

4
00:00:37,056 --> 00:00:52,056
This lecture follows all seven school sections, including the
treatment-failure loop. The separate airway-device lesson develops
insertion and suction techniques; memorizing a device name is not a
substitute for these functional decisions.

5
00:00:52,056 --> 00:01:06,240
The upper route conducts and conditions air. Loss of tone can let soft
tissues obstruct the pharynx; swelling or foreign material can
obstruct a different level. A visible mouth does not prove a patent
route to the lungs.

6
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:20,376
The tracheobronchial tree distributes gas. Smooth-muscle narrowing,
swelling and secretions increase resistance, particularly in smaller
airways. The work required to move the same gas volume rises.

7
00:01:20,376 --> 00:01:32,232
Alveoli provide a thin exchange surface beside flowing capillary
blood. Ventilation must reach that surface and perfusion must reach
the same region for effective gas exchange.

8
00:01:32,232 --> 00:01:45,696
The pump can fail even when the airway is open: depressed drive,
muscle fatigue, weakness or a disrupted chest can reduce effective
breaths. Examine the whole route and pump, not only breath sounds.

9
00:01:45,696 --> 00:02:00,384
During ordinary inspiration, diaphragm contraction increases thoracic
volume and lowers alveolar pressure below atmospheric pressure. Air
moves down the resulting pressure gradient, not because the lungs
actively suck on their own.

10
00:02:00,384 --> 00:02:14,592
At rest, expiration largely follows elastic recoil. Obstructed airways
can need longer to empty; another forced breath before exhalation
finishes can trap gas and increase intrathoracic pressure.

11
00:02:14,592 --> 00:02:29,928
Resistance concerns opposition to airflow. Compliance concerns volume
change for a pressure change. A stiff, poorly compliant lung and a
narrowed airway can both be hard to ventilate, but they are not the
same mechanism.

12
00:02:29,928 --> 00:02:44,208
Positive-pressure support can improve ventilation or recruitment but
may reduce venous return. Greater bag force is not automatically
better oxygen delivery, especially in shock or dynamic hyperinflation.

13
00:02:44,208 --> 00:02:59,088
Use a simplified adult teaching example with an assumed dead-space
volume of zero point one five litres per breath. These numbers
illustrate a relationship; they are not a patient-specific measurement
or a prescribed tidal volume.

14
00:02:59,088 --> 00:03:13,320
At twenty breaths of half a litre, minute ventilation is ten litres
and estimated alveolar ventilation is seven litres per minute. The
portion remaining in conducting dead space does not exchange gas in
the same way.

15
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:27,840
At forty quarter-litre breaths, minute ventilation is still ten
litres, but estimated alveolar ventilation falls to four litres. A
high rate cannot be interpreted without the depth and effectiveness of
each breath.

16
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:44,352
This explains why respiratory rate alone can falsely reassure. Observe
chest movement, air entry, effort, mental state and the carbon-dioxide
trend; do not calculate a normal-looking minute volume from guessed
bedside volumes.

17
00:03:44,352 --> 00:03:57,864
A person sitting forward and speaking in short phrases is giving
functional information before the stethoscope is applied. Observe work
of breathing and the ability to sustain it, not just the displayed
oxygen saturation.

18
00:03:57,864 --> 00:04:13,752
Compare both sides and several appropriate chest locations on skin.
Note air entry, added sounds and any asymmetry. Clothing, a noisy
vehicle and an incomplete examination must be recognized as
limitations.

19
00:04:13,752 --> 00:04:31,152
Ask about sudden versus gradual onset, chest discomfort, fever, cough,
swelling, exposures, trauma and prior episodes. Confirm actual inhaler
or oxygen use rather than inferring a diagnosis from equipment beside
the patient.

20
00:04:31,152 --> 00:04:45,816
Use a baseline and repeat the relevant findings after intervention. If
ventilation is immediately inadequate, treatment and a concise
assessment occur together; the full history can follow without
abandoning reassessment.

21
00:04:45,816 --> 00:05:01,536
Respiratory distress describes increased difficulty and effort.
Failure means the respiratory system cannot adequately perform its
oxygenation or ventilation task. The two can overlap, and failure does
not always look dramatic.

22
00:05:01,536 --> 00:05:16,632
A severely distressed patient who becomes quiet, sleepy and shallow
may be exhausting rather than recovering. A rate falling from thirty-
four to ten is not good news if chest expansion and responsiveness
also decline.

23
00:05:16,632 --> 00:05:32,112
Sedatives or opioids can suppress drive without an earlier period of
marked effort. Examine airway integrity, breath depth and the
circulation; a normal saturation on oxygen can coexist with dangerous
carbon-dioxide retention.

24
00:05:32,112 --> 00:05:45,120
Escalate support and resources when function fails. Do not leave an
inadequately ventilating patient on CPAP simply because the mask was
already fitted or its saturation initially improved.

25
00:05:45,120 --> 00:06:00,840
The school oxygen cascade moves from available environmental oxygen to
cellular use. Treat it as a causal map: a problem upstream can deprive
every later step, while a problem downstream may be invisible to a
saturation probe.

26
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:15,192
An obstructed airway limits entry. Pneumonia or edema can impair
exchange. Hemorrhage and anemia reduce effective oxygen carriage,
while poor cardiac output limits delivery of the oxygenated blood.

27
00:06:15,192 --> 00:06:30,144
Some toxic exposures impair cellular use, and sepsis can disturb
several steps together. More oxygen concentration cannot by itself
restore missing blood flow or make a poisoned cell use oxygen
normally.

28
00:06:30,144 --> 00:06:45,000
Name your evidence and uncertainty: poor ventilation, possible lung-
exchange failure or possible shock. Multiple links may fail at once,
so do not stop the assessment after finding the first abnormal sound.

29
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:59,112
Stridor suggests turbulent flow through a narrowed upper airway.
Sudden onset during eating, progressive swelling and a traumatic neck
injury create different pathways; the sound alone does not identify
the cause.

30
00:06:59,112 --> 00:07:13,872
Snoring can accompany loss of pharyngeal tone; gurgling or wet sounds
may indicate material needing appropriate clearance. Reassess after
positioning, a manual maneuver or suction rather than assuming the
airway is fixed.

31
00:07:13,872 --> 00:07:28,920
Treat severe obstruction urgently with the applicable cause-specific
procedure and help. Avoid repeated unnecessary stimulation or blind
finger sweeps, and do not force a patient with a threatened airway
into an intolerable position.

32
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:42,840
A reduction in noise can mean better patency or less airflow. Confirm
movement, air entry and patient function before declaring improvement;
a silent obstructed patient may be deteriorating.

33
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:56,640
Wheeze often reflects airflow through narrowed intrathoracic airways,
but it is not an asthma certificate. History, exposure, fever, edema
and other findings may support competing explanations.

34
00:07:56,640 --> 00:08:09,456
Observe the prolonged expiratory phase and whether the patient has
time to empty the lungs. Increasing resistance and effort can progress
to fatigue even while a saturation reading looks acceptable.

35
00:08:09,456 --> 00:08:24,480
A silent chest in a severely breathless person may reflect very little
airflow. Compare air entry, chest movement, speech and responsiveness;
do not use disappearance of wheeze alone as a treatment endpoint.

36
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:40,176
Provide the indicated authorized cause-specific therapy while
supporting breathing. If spontaneous ventilation fails, move to
effective assisted ventilation and escalation rather than repeating a
previously chosen intervention indefinitely.

37
00:08:40,176 --> 00:08:53,064
Crackles are discontinuous sounds associated with several processes,
including fluid or reopening of small airways. Their presence helps
form a pattern, not a unique diagnosis.

38
00:08:53,064 --> 00:09:08,736
An orthopneic patient with cardiac history and diffuse findings may
have cardiogenic edema. Fever, cough and focal findings may suggest
infection, but absence of fever does not exclude it and both
conditions can coexist.

39
00:09:08,736 --> 00:09:22,560
Assess pressure and perfusion before choosing a positive-pressure
strategy. A patient who is alert and maintaining ventilation differs
from one becoming hypotensive, exhausted or unable to protect the
airway.

40
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:35,472
CPAP can recruit alveoli and reduce work in suitable patients. It does
not literally squeeze all water out of the lungs, treat every cause or
remove the need for medication and repeated assessment.

41
00:09:35,472 --> 00:09:51,552
After trauma, inspect for asymmetry, wounds and abnormal movement,
compare air entry and assess perfusion. A changing respiratory
examination plus shock is more concerning than one uncertain
auscultation finding.

42
00:09:51,552 --> 00:10:03,552
Possible pneumothorax or other thoracic injury requires a time-
critical cause-specific pathway. Do not delay escalation or transport
while trying to prove a complete textbook triad.

43
00:10:03,552 --> 00:10:18,792
The school narrative mentions decompression as an advanced response.
That is not an independent permission for every PCP student to perform
it. Training, licence, employer authorization and the actual procedure
determine who can act.

44
00:10:18,792 --> 00:10:30,624
Suspected pneumothorax and traumatic respiratory distress also alter
the safety of pressure support. Do not transfer a non-traumatic CPAP
plan unchanged to this patient.

45
00:10:30,624 --> 00:10:45,744
For the school comparison exercise, retain its ninety-four to ninety-
eight and eighty-eight to ninety-two target ranges. Current BCEHS A
seven uses different operational wording; learn which source the
question or clinical setting is using.

46
00:10:45,744 --> 00:10:57,936
Titration means assessing the patient, delivering an appropriate
concentration and rechecking response. A NEWS2 zero-point saturation
band is not itself an oxygen prescription.

47
00:10:57,936 --> 00:11:15,000
Do not explain oxygen-associated carbon-dioxide rise solely as
switching off a hypoxic drive. Changes in ventilation-perfusion
matching and carbon-dioxide carriage also matter. Controlled therapy
is not a reason to abandon severe hypoxemia.

48
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:29,904
If breathing is inadequate, oxygen alone cannot provide ventilation.
Support the failing function and reassess consciousness, chest
movement, oxygenation and carbon dioxide while following the
applicable pathway.

49
00:11:29,904 --> 00:11:45,888
A low-flow nasal cannula allows speech and can support titrated
oxygen, but the actual inspired concentration varies with breathing
pattern, leaks and device flow. Do not convert a flow setting into a
guaranteed inspired percentage.

50
00:11:45,888 --> 00:12:00,192
A reservoir mask requires the appropriate manufacturer flow and a
functioning reservoir, valves and seal. A poorly inflated reservoir or
disconnected supply can make a reassuring mask appearance misleading.

51
00:12:00,192 --> 00:12:14,088
No passive oxygen device compensates for an apneic patient. Open and
clear the airway, provide effective ventilation when indicated, and
choose another device only if it solves the actual remaining problem.

52
00:12:14,088 --> 00:12:29,184
Estimate supply requirements for transport, including high oxygen
consumption by some pressure devices. Recheck flow, connections and
patient effect after movement, cylinder change and any unexpected
deterioration.

53
00:12:29,184 --> 00:12:41,688
A capnogram displays exhaled carbon dioxide over time. First identify
a repeatable respiratory waveform and a functioning sampling system
before interpreting its end-tidal number.

54
00:12:41,688 --> 00:12:56,544
Early expired conducting gas has little carbon dioxide, followed by
the expiratory upstroke and alveolar plateau. The end-tidal value is
measured near the end of that plateau; inspiration produces the
descending limb.

55
00:12:56,544 --> 00:13:11,184
The school thirty-five to forty-five millimetre range is a reference,
not a universal target for every condition. End-tidal and arterial
carbon dioxide can diverge when dead space, perfusion or lung disease
changes.

56
00:13:11,184 --> 00:13:27,264
Use waveform shape, trend and examination together. A missing waveform
can mean absent ventilation, disconnection, obstruction, displacement,
low perfusion or sampling failure; assess and troubleshoot
immediately.

57
00:13:27,264 --> 00:13:43,176
With reduced effective alveolar ventilation, carbon dioxide can
accumulate and the end-tidal value may rise. A drowsy patient with
shallow breaths and a rising trace needs functional breathing
assessment, not simply oxygen adjustment.

58
00:13:43,176 --> 00:13:59,568
A falling value can follow improved ventilation, but can also indicate
falling pulmonary blood flow. In a patient becoming pale and
hypotensive, a lower end-tidal number may be a circulation warning
rather than a respiratory success.

59
00:13:59,568 --> 00:14:13,008
Obstructive disease may produce a sloping shark-fin pattern, but
leaks, sampling and mixed disease affect the trace. A descriptive
pattern supports reasoning; it does not replace the rest of the
examination.

60
00:14:13,008 --> 00:14:26,976
Capnography often changes before pulse oximetry in hypoventilation,
especially with oxygen therapy. There is no fixed universal thirty-to-
sixty-second lag to memorize for every device and patient.

61
00:14:26,976 --> 00:14:41,928
During resuscitation, pulmonary blood flow strongly influences exhaled
carbon dioxide. Compression-generated flow is one contributor, but
ventilation, the airway, metabolism and measurement also affect the
number.

62
00:14:41,928 --> 00:14:54,120
The school highlights ten and twenty millimetres as teaching markers.
Do not convert them into a stand-alone pass-fail test for compressions
or an independent rule to stop resuscitation.

63
00:14:54,120 --> 00:15:07,848
An abrupt sustained rise can suggest improved circulation or return of
spontaneous circulation. Correlate it with the appropriate clinical
and rhythm assessment without creating unnecessary compression
interruptions.

64
00:15:07,848 --> 00:15:20,832
If the value falls unexpectedly, verify the patient and airway circuit
and review ventilation and compression quality. Treat the cause, not a
target number detached from the resuscitation context.

65
00:15:20,832 --> 00:15:35,952
A bag can be squeezed while no effective volume reaches the lungs.
Check the equipment, position, airway patency and mask seal before
increasing force. Use an airway adjunct when indicated and compatible
with the patient.

66
00:15:35,952 --> 00:15:49,800
Where two providers are available, one maintains the airway and two-
handed mask seal while the other gives breaths and watches the chest.
Lift the jaw toward the mask rather than pressing the mask hard into
soft tissue.

67
00:15:49,800 --> 00:16:04,608
Deliver only enough volume for a slight definite chest rise, allow
exhalation and monitor effectiveness. Excess volume, pressure or rate
can inflate the stomach, increase aspiration risk and impair
circulation.

68
00:16:04,608 --> 00:16:18,816
If the chest does not rise, correct patency and seal, inspect for
obstruction and equipment problems, and escalate the airway plan if
needed. Repeated failed squeezes do not count as successful
ventilation.

69
00:16:18,816 --> 00:16:34,536
The school gives ten to twelve adult breaths per minute as general BVM
teaching. The named twenty-twenty-five AHA adult respiratory-arrest
branch, with a definite pulse, specifies one breath every six seconds,
or ten per minute.

70
00:16:34,536 --> 00:16:50,088
For the latter branch, use visible chest rise and reassess pulse every
two minutes. A patient without a definite pulse and without an
advanced airway follows the compression-and-breath sequence, not
uninterrupted rescue breaths alone.

71
00:16:50,088 --> 00:17:04,320
These numbers are not an instruction to ignore spontaneous breaths or
force a single rate in every illness. Coordinate assisted breaths
appropriately and allow exhalation, especially with obstructive
physiology.

72
00:17:04,320 --> 00:17:19,296
Pediatric rates, advanced-airway CPR and the specific PEEP procedure
have separate instructions. State the patient and branch before
quoting a rate, and use the current school resuscitation standard for
its supervised skills check.

73
00:17:19,296 --> 00:17:33,168
PEEP retains pressure at the end of expiration. CPAP provides
continuous pressure during spontaneous breathing. Neither name
guarantees effective ventilation or eliminates the need to examine
airway integrity.

74
00:17:33,168 --> 00:17:49,968
Current PR ten starts indicated BVM PEEP at five centimetres of water,
with monitored adjustments of two point five up to ten. The procedure
has specific exclusions and requires consultation for persistent
hypoxemia despite maximal therapy.

75
00:17:49,968 --> 00:18:06,096
That procedure excludes cardiac arrest, traumatic respiratory arrest,
suspected pneumothorax and defined low-pressure states. Its usual
eight-to-ten breath wording is context-specific, not a reason to
overwrite every other ventilation algorithm.

76
00:18:06,096 --> 00:18:21,720
Monitor pressure, chest mechanics and oxygenation, and discontinue
when a contraindication or equipment concern develops. Pressure is a
treatment with physiological trade-offs, not an automatic upgrade for
every breathless patient.

77
00:18:21,720 --> 00:18:34,368
CPAP helps a suitable spontaneously breathing patient; it does not
generate mandatory breaths. The most important first question is
whether the person can maintain the airway and keep ventilating.

78
00:18:34,368 --> 00:18:47,352
For the current PR nine selection exercise, check the listed age,
distress findings and supplemental-oxygen context together. An
isolated low room-air saturation is not the whole indication.

79
00:18:47,352 --> 00:18:58,392
Authorization and training are part of the decision, not a separate
administrative detail. A lecture about CPAP cannot authorize a learner
to apply it independently.

80
00:18:58,392 --> 00:19:10,608
Explain the aim, obtain applicable consent and plan what you will do
if it fails before fitting the device. The patient must be able to
report intolerance and cooperate with close reassessment.

81
00:19:10,608 --> 00:19:24,744
In the public PR nine procedure, the listed exclusions must be checked
before treatment and again if the condition changes. Failure to follow
commands is not just a comfort problem; it undermines safe use.

82
00:19:24,744 --> 00:19:38,544
A patient with slower shallow breaths and falling consciousness needs
effective ventilation, not a tighter CPAP strap. Remove an ineffective
or unsafe strategy and enact the prepared airway-support plan.

83
00:19:38,544 --> 00:19:51,432
Vomiting or inability to protect the airway creates an aspiration
concern. Anticipate access to suction and an alternate support method
rather than waiting until secretions cannot be cleared.

84
00:19:51,432 --> 00:20:04,944
Use the full examination to distinguish anxiety with maintained
function from evolving failure. Reassurance can improve tolerance, but
it must not be used to explain away physiological deterioration.

85
00:20:04,944 --> 00:20:17,736
The PR nine fixed-flow device starts at five litres per minute if
possible, otherwise six. That is a device-linked setup, not a
universal conversion between litres per minute and centimetres of
water.

86
00:20:17,736 --> 00:20:34,248
Let the patient hold the mask initially and build the seal
progressively with explanation. Once tolerated, secure it and recheck
leaks and effect; follow the manufacturer chart, never exceeding ten
centimetres of water under PR nine.

87
00:20:34,248 --> 00:20:48,480
Do not attach a BVM to a CPAP mask as a substitute circuit, and do not
delay indicated medication to fit it. Equipment-specific nebulizer
connections and oxygen changes require the actual device procedure.

88
00:20:48,480 --> 00:21:06,696
Reassess the whole patient after application and each change: effort,
volume, mental state, oxygenation and circulation. A brief initial
saturation change needs interpretation, but ongoing deterioration
requires action, not passive waiting.

89
00:21:06,696 --> 00:21:21,720
In this new simulation, an initially distressed adult becomes quieter
during treatment. The oxygen saturation remains ninety-five percent on
supplemental oxygen, but the patient is drowsier and moving much less
air.

90
00:21:21,720 --> 00:21:35,760
The correct explanation is not that the wheeze and rate improved. The
combined fall in responsiveness, rate and tidal movement indicates
loss of effective ventilation; carbon-dioxide rise supports that
concern.

91
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:48,912
Return to airway and breathing support, obtain additional help and use
effective assisted ventilation within the applicable pathway. Preserve
enough expiratory time and reassess for pressure-related harm.

92
00:21:48,912 --> 00:22:01,608
State what changed, what you did and what happened next. That
explanation demonstrates recognition of a transition from distress to
failure; listing a bronchodilator name alone does not.

93
00:22:01,608 --> 00:22:16,488
An adult with the displayed initial findings may satisfy the
physiological PR nine criteria, but that is only after checking the
rest of the history, examination and exclusions. The case is original,
not a school assessment answer.

94
00:22:16,488 --> 00:22:30,720
A trained authorized provider could select CPAP with close monitoring
and appropriate concurrent care. Predict an improvement in work,
oxygenation and maintained mentation rather than claiming the device
cures the underlying disease.

95
00:22:30,720 --> 00:22:45,384
Now change one variable group: the patient becomes drowsy with eight
shallow breaths per minute. The treatment assumptions fail. This is a
ventilation and airway-support problem even if a saturation has not
yet fallen.

96
00:22:45,384 --> 00:22:58,464
Stop treating the original indication as permanent. Reassess
immediately, provide the indicated alternate support and escalate
resources, communicating the deterioration and actual response.

97
00:22:58,464 --> 00:23:13,680
An unresponsive adult has five shallow breaths per minute, a definite
pulse and a high saturation while receiving oxygen. Possible opioid
exposure is relevant, but the decisive initial finding is inadequate
ventilation.

98
00:23:13,680 --> 00:23:24,504
Open and clear the airway and provide effective assisted breaths.
Waiting for the saturation to fall or for naloxone to work leaves a
failing function unsupported.

99
00:23:24,504 --> 00:23:37,128
Give naloxone when indicated and authorized, using the actual route
and schedule taught in the medication lesson. The aim is adequate
breathing, not forcing complete wakefulness at any cost.

100
00:23:37,128 --> 00:23:52,584
Reassess sustained breathing, circulation and other possible causes.
Recurrent depression can occur after an initial response; a transient
improvement is not permission to end monitoring or make an unsupported
non-conveyance decision.

101
00:23:52,584 --> 00:24:05,304
Record what was happening before the intervention and the support
already in place. A saturation without oxygen-device context and a
respiratory rate without depth lose important meaning.

102
00:24:05,304 --> 00:24:21,096
Compare work, speech, chest movement, air entry, mental state,
perfusion, saturation and appropriate waveform trends. Improvement
should be coherent across relevant findings, not merely one selected
number.

103
00:24:21,096 --> 00:24:34,968
An unexpected response should reopen the differential and the
equipment check. Consider disease progression, an incorrect
assumption, a leak, obstruction, depleted oxygen or a pressure-related
adverse effect.

104
00:24:34,968 --> 00:24:50,376
Handover must include baseline, change, device or medication details
and remaining concern. Saying respiratory patient, better after oxygen
hides the reasoning and may obscure ongoing ventilatory failure.

105
00:24:50,376 --> 00:25:04,032
In the school station, state your role and identify the immediate
respiratory problem before choosing a device. Prepare the equipment
and a fallback, and assign a second provider when the skill benefits
from one.

106
00:25:04,032 --> 00:25:17,928
Demonstrate a patent airway and effective bag-mask technique on the
appropriate trainer. Then explain how you distinguish an alert,
spontaneously ventilating CPAP candidate from a patient needing
assisted ventilation.

107
00:25:17,928 --> 00:25:32,856
Have the observer change one feature: a leak, weaker breathing, a
falling pressure or a disconnected sampling line. State the finding,
correct the cause and verify the actual effect rather than announcing
the skill complete.

108
00:25:32,856 --> 00:25:47,016
Use the original practice questions and paired reading to explain the
decisions without notes. Physical competence, patient safety and the
school skills sign-off still require supervised demonstration, not
playback completion.
